\v 30 A man must never \add ≈sleep with\add* any of his father's wives \add ?≈because that would expose his father's nakedness\add*.\x + \xo 22:30: \xt Lev 18:8; 20:11; Deu 27:20.\x*
\c 23
\s1 Excluded people
\rem /s1 Exclusion From the Assembly; Regulations concerning Worship; Those Excluded from the Assembly; Exclusion from the Lord's People
\p
\v 1 \add ≈No man¦92871 with crushed testicles\add* or \add ≈a severed penis\add* is allowed to enter into Yahweh's assembly.
\p
\v 2 No \add ?≈illegitimate child\add*\f + \fr 23:2 \ft Or possibly, child from mixed parentage.\f* is¦92886 allowed to enter into¦92891 Yahweh's¦92892 assembly¦92891, even down to the¦92891 tenth generation.
\p
\v 3 No Ammonite or Moabite is allowed to¦92908 enter¦92898 into¦92900 Yahweh's¦92901 assembly¦92909, even down past the¦92900 tenth¦92904 generation¦92903\x + \xo 23:3-5: \xt Neh 13:1-2.\x*
\v 4 since they wouldn't \add even sell\add* you bread and¦92918 water when the¦92919 people were travelling from Egypt \add to Canaan\add*. \add ≈What's more,\add* they hired Beor's son Bil'am \add !(Balaam)\add* (from Petor in¦92919 Aram-Naharayim) to¦92927 curse you
\v 5 \add ≈However,\add* your god Yahweh \add ≈took no notice of¦92937\add* Bil'am and¦92944 turned the¦92954 curse¦92960 into a blessing because \add *he¦92949\add* loves you,\x + \xo 23:5: \xt Num 23:7–24:9.\x*
\v 6 \add ≈so\add* you¦92975 must \add ≈never\add* strive for peace or prosperity for \add @the¦92965 Ammonites¦92965 or¦92965 the¦92965 Moabites¦92965\add*.
\p
\v 7 \add In contrast,\add* don't despise the Edomites because they're your¦92993 \add ≈cousins\add* \add (descendants of your ancestor Yitshak)\add*. And¦92990 don't despise the Egyptians, because you¦92988 \add ≈lived there\add* as foreigners.
\v 8 The third generation \add !(grandchildren)\add* of any of \add @Edomites¦93004 or¦93004 Egyptians¦93004\add* that live among you¦93000, may enter Yahweh's assembly.
\s1 Purity when at war
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Regulations; Keeping the Military Camp Clean; Uncleanness in the Camp; Sanitary, Ritual, and Humanitarian Precepts
\p
\v 9 When¦93027 \add @your¦93020 warriors¦93020\add* are¦93020 out besieging your enemies, they¦93020 you must¦93020 avoid \add ≈ceremonial impurity\add*.
\v 10 If¦93031 something happens at night to make a man ‘unclean’, then he must¦93033 stay outside¦93033 the camp¦93034 \add the next day\add* \add ≈and¦93038 not join the others\add*.
\v 11 That¦93049 evening, he must¦93046 wash himself in¦93047 water, then once the¦93060 sun has set, he¦93063 may reenter the camp¦93067.
\p
\v 12 You must¦93074 have a place outside your camp¦93082 where you can go¦93076 to¦93071 \add relieve yourself\add*.
\v 13 You¦93091 must¦93087 have a spade among your equipment so that when you go¦93091 to¦93088 relieve yourself¦93088, you can dig a hole and¦93086 then cover it¦93087 up afterwards,
\v 14 because¦93098 your¦93101 god Yahweh walks in¦93103 your camp to give you victory \add ≈to defeat\add* your enemies, so your camp must¦93097 be¦93102 sacred so he won't see anything ‘unclean’ and¦93102 turn¦93107 away from you¦93108.
\s1 Various regulations
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Laws; Various Laws
\p
\v 15 You¦93115 mustn't \add ≈return¦93134\add* any slaves to their master if¦93114 they escaped and¦93122 got to you¦93135.
\v 16 \add ≈Allow them to¦93143 stay\add* \add ≈in your town\add* and don't¦93139 \add ≈mistreat\add* them.
\p
\v 17 \add ≈Don't¦93165 allow¦93155 any Israeli man¦93161 or woman to¦93164 become a religious\add*\f + \fr 23:17 \ft Or, cultic.\f* prostitute.\x + \xo 23:17: \xt Lev 19:29.\x*
\v 18 \add ≈Don't¦93170 allow anyone woman¦93174 or man to bring any¦93174 part of¦93181 their wages\add* as a \add secular\add* prostitute¦93173 into your god Yahweh's temple, because both \add @female¦93173 and¦93176 male¦93179 prostitution¦93179\add* is abhorrent to \add *him\add*.
\p
\v 19 You¦93187 mustn't charge interest to¦93195 your¦93201 fellow Israeli, whether it's money or food or anything else \add ≈that's borrowed\add*.\x + \xo 23:19-20: \xt Exo 22:25; Lev 25:36-37; Deu 15:7-11.\x*
\v 20 You¦93209 can charge¦93209 foreigners interest¦93209, but not¦93207 \add fellow\add* Israelis so that your¦93210 god Yahweh will¦93220 bless \add @your society\add* in everything you¦93209 do in the land that you're about¦93210 to¦93210 occupy.
\s1 Making promises to God
\p
\v 21 When you¦93224 make a vow to¦93225 your¦93225 god¦93231 Yahweh¦93230, don't¦93226 put off fulfilling it¦93244, because \add *he¦93229\add* will¦93224 \add ≈hold you accountable\add* and¦93225 \add not doing it\add* would¦93227 \add count as\add* \add ≈disobeying him\add*.
\v 22 However \add of course\add*, \add ≈if¦93248 you¦93250 don't¦93254 make any vows, then you won't¦93254 be¦93264 guilty\add*,
\v 23 \add ≈but¦93269\add* if \add ≈you've spoken your promise out loud\add* to your god Yahweh, then¦93269 you're \add ≈obligated to¦93271 keep it¦93274\add*.
\p
\v 24 If¦93285 you're walking through \add ≈someone else's\add* vineyard, you can eat as many grapes as \add ≈you like\add*, but¦93282 you mustn't pick¦93281 any to¦93285 \add take away\add* in¦93290 a container.
\v 25 \add ≈Similarly,\add* if¦93294 you're going¦93295 through \add ≈someone else's\add* field of grain, you can \add crunch\add* \add ≈a few kernels, but¦93298\add* you mustn't \add ≈cut off any of the¦93296 stalks\add*.
\c 24
\s1 Divorce and remarriage
\rem /s1 Divorce and Remarriage; Laws concerning Marriage and Divorce
\p
\v 1 When a man¦93328 marries¦93330 a woman¦93329, then \add ≈further on¦93338 he decides he doesn't want her because¦93325 of¦93338 something he¦93341 found¦93341 out about¦93338\add* her, then he must¦93327 write¦93345 a divorce¦93348 notice and¦93349 give¦93349 it¦93349 to¦93346 her, and¦93351 send¦93351 her out of his¦93338 house¦93352.\x + \xo 24:1: \xt Mat 5:31; 19:7; Mrk 10:4.\x*
\v 2 \add ≈After she¦93354 leaves¦93354 that marriage\add*, \add ≈another¦93360 man¦93358 is allowed to¦93358 marry her\add*.
\v 3 If¦93374 that \add ≈second\add* husband \add also\add* \add ≈decides to divorce her by sending¦93371 her¦93371 away¦93371 with¦93370 a divorce notice\add*, or¦93373 if¦93374 he¦93375 were to die¦93375,
\v 4 then the¦93388 first¦93388 husband¦93387 isn't¦93406 allow to take¦93393 her¦93387 back as his wife¦93396. \add That's\add* because¦93400 she's¦93399 been¦93399 defiled¦93399 and it¦93403 would be¦93394 abhorrent to Yahweh, and you mustn't defile¦93399 the land¦93410 that¦93411 your¦93413 god¦93413 Yahweh is¦93414 giving¦93414 you.
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Laws; Various Laws
\p
\v 5 When a man¦93422 \add ≈is newly married\add*, he¦93421 mustn't \add ≈be called¦93419 for\add* the army¦93427 \add ≈or be given any¦93432 other responsibilities\add*. He¦93436 must¦93421 be free¦93435 for one¦93439 year¦93438 \add ≈to establish his house¦93437\add* and¦93440 \add ≈to satisfy his new¦93424\add* wife¦93423.
\p
\v 6 \add If someone borrows money,\add* \add @the lender\add* mustn't take¦93451 their millstone \add for grinding grain\add* as¦93458 a pledge¦93458, because¦93454 \add ≈they need it¦93457 to stay alive¦93456\add*.
\s1 Abduction
\p
\v 7 If¦93461 someone¦93464 is¦93463 found¦93463 to have abducted a fellow Israeli¦93469 to make them into a slave¦93470 or to sell¦93473 them, then that¦93476 abductor must¦93463 \add ≈be¦93463 executed\add* in¦93472 order to purge¦93477 such evils¦93478 from¦93479 \add ≈your¦93479 country¦93467\add*.\x + \xo 24:7: \xt Exo 21:16.\x*
\s1 Skin diseases
\p
\v 8 Stay on¦93482 guard¦93485 against a \add ≈deadly skin¦93484 disease¦93484\add*. Always follow what the¦93482 priests¦93493 and¦93487 Levites¦93494 tell you all¦93488 to¦93482 do¦93487, as¦93495 I've instructed¦93491 them¦93496.\x + \xo 24:8: \xt Lev 13:1–14:54.\x*
\v 9 Remember¦93501 what your¦93507 god¦93507 Yahweh¦93506 did¦93505 to¦93508 Miryam on¦93509 the way¦93509 out¦93510 of¦93507 Egypt¦93511 \add (Heb. Mitsrayim)\add*.\x + \xo 24:9: \xt Num 12:9-10.\x*
\p
\v 10 If¦93514 you've lent¦93516 anything¦93520 to a neighbour¦93517, don't¦93521 go¦93523 into¦93524 their house¦93526 to¦93527 collect¦93527 their pledge¦93527.
\v 11 \add Just\add* \add ≈wait\add* outside¦93530, then the¦93532 \add ≈person¦93532\add* you've lent¦93535 something to will¦93535 bring¦93537 the \add ≈item\add* out¦93537 to¦93538 you¦93538.
\v 12 \add ≈However,\add* if¦93544 \add ≈that person¦93546\add* is¦93546 poor¦93547 \add and gives clothing or a blanket as security\add*, don't¦93549 \add ≈keep it overnight\add*.
\v 13 Take it¦93555 back to¦93555 them when the¦93558 sun¦93560 goes¦93559 down¦93561 so they'll \add be warm\add* when they sleep¦93561. Then they'll bless¦93563 you¦93563 and¦93564 your¦93569 god¦93569 Yahweh¦93568 \add ≈will¦93554 be pleased with¦93562\add* you¦93564.
\p
\v 14 Don't¦93572 oppress¦93574 any poor¦93576 or needy¦93577 hired¦93575 workers, whether they're fellow Israelis or¦93579 foreigners who¦93581 live in¦93582 your¦93580 towns¦93583.\x + \xo 24:14-15: \xt Lev 19:13.\x*
\v 15 At the¦93592 end of¦93587 each day¦93585, pay¦93586 them before sunset, because¦93593 they need it¦93596 to live on¦93605. Then they won't¦93588 cry¦93604 out¦93604 to¦93606 Yahweh¦93608 against¦93605 you and¦93609 have him¦93591 \add ≈hold you accountable\add*.
\p
\v 16 Parents¦93617 mustn't be executed for what their children¦93620 have done, and¦93621 children mustn't be executed for what their parents¦93617 have done. Any person must¦93616 only be executed for what they¦93616 themselves have done.\x + \xo 24:16: \xt 2Ki 14:6; 2Ch 25:4; Eze 18:20.\x*
\p
\v 17 Don't¦93636 pervert justice¦93638 if it involves foreigners¦93639 or orphans¦93640, and¦93641 don't¦93636 take¦93642 a widow's¦93644 cloak as¦93642 security \add for a loan\add*.\x + \xo 24:17-18: \xt Exo 23:9; Lev 19:33-34; Deu 27:19.\x*
\v 18 Remember¦93646 that¦93664 you were¦93649 a slave¦93648 in¦93650 Egypt¦93650 and¦93646 your¦93659 god¦93653 Yahweh¦93652 \add ≈rescued\add* you from¦93654 there¦93654. \add ≈That's¦93647 why\add* I'm¦93658 commanding¦93659 you¦93659 to do¦93660 those things¦93663—\add ≈to be considerate about¦93653 helping other people who are¦93659 in need\add*.
\p
\v 19 When you¦93668 \add ≈harvest¦93668 your crops\add*, if¦93667 you¦93671 forget¦93671 a tied bundle in¦93670 your¦93670 field¦93673, don't¦93674 go back to¦93676 get¦93676 it—it'll¦93680 be for a \add resident\add* foreigner or an orphan¦93678 or widow¦93679 \add to pick up\add*, so¦93681 that¦93681 your god¦93684 Yahweh¦93683 can bless¦93682 everything \add ≈that you¦93682 do\add*.\x + \xo 24:19-21: \xt Lev 19:9-10; 23:22.\x*
\v 20 When you¦93690 beat¦93690 your¦93691 olive¦93691 tree \add to make the fruit fall\add*, don't¦93692 go¦93693 back over¦93693 it¦93698 to pick the ones still on the tree. Those will¦93698 be for the foreigner, or the orphan¦93696 or widow¦93697.
\v 21 \add Similarly,\add* when you¦93702 \add ≈pick your¦93703 grapes¦93702\add* in the vineyard¦93703, don't¦93704 \add ≈go back over the vines a second time\add*. Those will¦93702 be for¦93701 the foreigner¦93707, or the orphan¦93708 or widow¦93709.
\v 22 \add ^Don't forget\add* that¦93728 you were¦93716 a slave¦93715 in¦93717 Egypt¦93718—that's¦93713 why I'm¦93722 instructing you¦93723 to do¦93724 those things¦93727.
\c 25
\s1 Whipping for criminals
\p
\v 1 If¦93731 \add ≈two parties have a dispute¦93734, they¦93737 can request a judgement¦93740 and the¦93740 judges¦93741 will¦93741 decide¦93741 who's right¦93742 and¦93746 wrong\add*.
\v 2 If¦93752 \add ≈the¦93756 guilty¦93756 person is¦93754 deserving of¦93754 punishment\add*, the¦93758 judge¦93758 will¦93759 order him¦93759 to¦93761 lie¦93757 on¦93763 the ground \add ≈where he'll¦93757 be whipped in¦93763 front¦93760 of the judge as the crime deserves\add*.
\v 3 He¦93766 may not¦93767 \add ≈receive more than forty¦93765 lashes¦93766\add* because that would be¦93778 \add ≈too degrading¦93778\add*.
\p
\v 4 Don't¦93783 \add ≈cover your cow's mouth when¦93787 it's¦93787 pulling the grain threshing¦93787 sledge\add*.\x + \xo 25:4: \xt 1Cor 9:9; 1Tim 5:18.\x*
\s1 Continuing the family name
\rem /s1 Levirate Marriage; Duty to a Dead Brother
\p
\v 5 If¦93790 some brothers¦93793 live¦93792 \add ≈on¦93813 the same property\add* and one¦93796 of them¦93797 dies¦93795 without leaving¦93812 a son¦93798, then the widow mustn't marry outside¦93808 \add ≈of¦93811 the family\add*. Her¦93811 brother-in-law must¦93792 take¦93814 her¦93811 as¦93817 a wife¦93805 for¦93816 himself and¦93817 \add ≈perform¦93817 the duty of\add* a brother-in-law¦93817.\x + \xo 25:5-6: \xt Mat 22:24; Mrk 12:19; Luk 20:28.\x*
\v 6 If she¦93822 gives birth¦93822 to a son \add in due course\add*, he'll¦93823 be¦93831 considered as the son of¦93826 the brother¦93827 who died¦93828, so that his¦93832 \add family\add* name¦93832 won't¦93829 disappear from¦93833 Yisrael¦93833.
\v 7 \add ≈But¦93835 if¦93835 the¦93839 dead man's¦93839 brother doesn't want¦93838 to¦93840 marry the widow\add*, she¦93844 must¦93838 go¦93844 to the elders¦93849 at¦93856 the city gate¦93846 and¦93835 \add ≈explain that her brother-in-law¦93852 is¦93858 refusing¦93851 to¦93853 do¦93859 his¦93854 duty to¦93859 preserve\add* the dead brother's¦93859 \add family\add* name¦93855 in¦93856 Yisrael¦93856.\x + \xo 25:7-10: \xt Rut 4:7-8.\x*
\v 8 Then¦93861 those elders¦93864 must¦93861 summon¦93861 \add @the¦93864 brother¦93864\add* and¦93861 talk¦93867 to¦93868 him¦93868. \add ≈However¦93872,\add* if he¦93869 \add ≈really\add* doesn't want¦93872 to¦93873 marry¦93873 her¦93873,
\v 9 then¦93875 his \add late\add* brother's wife¦93876 must go up to¦93877 him¦93877 in front¦93885 of¦93878 those elders¦93879, and¦93880 take his sandal¦93881 off¦93880 his¦93881 foot¦93883 and¦93884 spit¦93884 in his face¦93885. Then¦93875 she¦93886 must say¦93887, ‘That's what happens to¦93890 a man¦93890 who¦93891 won't¦93892 \add ≈strengthen his brother's family¦93897\add*.’
\v 10 \add After that,\add* the brother will¦93902 be¦93902 known in¦93904 Yisrael¦93904 as the man who had his¦93903 sandal¦93907 taken off¦93906.
\s1 Being fair and honest
\rem /s1 Various Commands; Other Laws
\p
\v 11 If¦93910 two men¦93913 are fighting¦93912 each other, and one¦93919 man's wife¦93918 comes¦93917 to¦93920 help her¦93927 husband¦93923, and¦93926 reaches out¦93926 and grabs the¦93918 other man's¦93925 private parts,
\v 12 then¦93931 you¦93931 must¦93931 cut¦93931 her hand¦93934 off¦93931. Don't¦93935 pity¦93936 her.
\s1 Don't all cheat
\p
\v 13 Don't¦93940 keep two stone \add weights\add* in¦93944 your¦93944 bag¦93944—a \add ≈lighter one\add* and a \add ≈heavier one\add* \add for when you're buying and selling\add*.\x + \xo 25:13-16: \xt Lev 19:35-36.\x*
\v 14 Don't¦93951 keep \add ≈two measuring¦93956 containers\add* in¦93955 your¦93955 house¦93955—a smaller¦93959 \add one\add* and¦93957 a larger¦93958 \add one\add*.
\v 15 You¦93966 must¦93964 use exact stone \add weights\add* and¦93969 \add ≈measuring¦93967 containers\add* so¦93973 that¦93973 your¦93975 god¦93981 Yahweh¦93980 will¦93964 prolong your time¦93975 in the¦93977 \add ≈country\add* that¦93978 he's giving¦93982 you—
\v 16 \add *he¦93987\add* \add ≈can't¦93991 stand¦93991 people¦93991 who¦93991 cheat¦93991 others¦93991\add*.
\s1 Eliminating the Amalekites
\rem /s1 The Command to Kill the Amalekites
\p
\v 17 \add ^Don't forget\add* what \add ≈the Amalekites¦94004\add* did¦94002 to¦94003 you \add people\add* when¦94006 you¦94006 came¦94006 out¦94006 of Egypt¦94007.\x + \xo 25:17-19: \xt Exo 17:8-14; 1Sam 15:2-9.\x*
\v 18 They met¦94010 you¦94010 on¦94011 the road¦94011, then attacked¦94012 you¦94013 from behind¦94017—\add getting\add* the stragglers¦94016 who¦94016 were¦94016 weak and¦94019 exhausted¦94020. They had no fear¦94023 of¦94014 God¦94024.
\v 19 \add ≈At some point in¦94027 the future when¦94027\add* your god¦94029 Yahweh¦94028 has given¦94027 you the¦94036 land¦94036 to occupy¦94044 as an¦94043 inheritance¦94043, and¦94026 \add ≈you're no longer being¦94026 attacked by your¦94029\add* enemies¦94034, you¦94045 must¦94045 \add ≈eliminate the¦94048 Amalekites¦94049 so they won't¦94052 be remembered¦94048 anymore\add*. Don't¦94052 forget¦94053 \add to do that\add*.
\c 26
\s1 Offerings for the beginning of the harvest
\rem /s1 Firstfruits and Tithes; Harvest Offerings and Tithes; Harvest Offerings; First Fruits and Tithes
\p
\v 1 \add ≈After\add* you've entered the¦94062 land¦94062 that¦94063 your¦94065 god¦94065 Yahweh¦94064 is¦94066 giving¦94066 you as an¦94068 inheritance¦94068, and¦94069 you¦94069 occupy¦94069 it¦94069 and¦94070 live¦94070 there,
\v 2 \add each of\add* you¦94073 should take the¦94074 first¦94074 part of your harvest in that place and¦94073 put¦94088 it in¦94089 a basket¦94089. Then¦94073 take it to the¦94093 place¦94093 that your god¦94097 Yahweh¦94084 is¦94086 going¦94090 to¦94091 choose¦94095 to¦94098 attach his¦94099 name¦94099 to.\x + \xo 26:2: \xt Exo 23:19.\x*
\v 3 Go¦94102 to¦94103 the¦94105 \add high\add* priest¦94105 \add ≈in¦94108 office¦94105\add* at that¦94106 time¦94108, and¦94102 tell¦94110 him¦94111, “I¦94112 declare¦94112 today to¦94114 your¦94115 god¦94115 Yahweh¦94114 that¦94116 I've¦94118 entered¦94118 the¦94121 land¦94121 that¦94122 \add *he¦94123\add* promised¦94123 to¦94125 our¦94125 ancestors¦94125 to¦94126 give¦94126 us¦94127.”
\p
\v 4 The¦94130 priest¦94130 will¦94133 take¦94129 that basket¦94131 of food and¦94129 place it¦94133 in¦94134 front¦94134 of your¦94137 god¦94137 Yahweh's¦94136 altar¦94135.
\v 5 Then¦94139 in¦94152 Yahweh's¦94143 presence¦94141 you¦94139 should respond¦94139, “My¦94147 ancestor¦94147 was¦94145 a wandering¦94146 Aramean¦94145, and¦94148 he¦94148 went down to¦94149 Egypt¦94149 with¦94152 his \add ≈smallish family\add*, and lived¦94150 there¦94151 and¦94154 \add eventually\add* became a large¦94158 and powerful¦94158 people¦94152.
\v 6 \add ≈But¦94162\add* the¦94164 Egyptians¦94164 treated¦94162 us badly and¦94162 oppressed¦94165 us¦94165—forcing us¦94167 into hard¦94169 labour¦94168.
\v 7 We¦94178 cried¦94171 out¦94171 to¦94172 our¦94176 ancestors¦94176' god¦94175 Yahweh¦94174 \add to help us\add*, and¦94177 he¦94177 heard¦94177 our cry¦94171 and¦94182 saw¦94182 our suffering and oppression¦94191 and the¦94175 \add hard\add* labour.
\v 8 Then¦94193 \add ≈using your great¦94201 power\add* and¦94202 \add ≈doing many miracles¦94202 and other terrifying¦94200 things\add*, you brought¦94193 us¦94193 out¦94193 from¦94195 Egypt¦94195
\v 9 and¦94205 brought¦94205 us¦94205 to¦94206 this¦94209 \add ≈very fertile\add* land¦94215
\v 10 \add ≈So¦94222 now¦94222\add*, Yahweh¦94234, I've¦94224 brought¦94224 the¦94227 first¦94227 part of¦94227 the harvest from that land¦94229 that¦94230 you've¦94232 given¦94232 me¦94233.”
\p Then¦94222 \add ≈as\add* the¦94236 basket is placed¦94235 in front of Yahweh, you¦94235 must¦94235 bow¦94239 down¦94239 in¦94240 front¦94240 of¦94240 \add *him¦94241\add*.
\v 11 Then¦94244 you¦94244 can celebrate¦94244 \add by eating\add* all¦94245 the¦94247 good¦94247 things that¦94248 your god¦94253 Yahweh¦94252 has¦94249 given¦94249 to¦94254 you and your household¦94254, and it's \add also\add* for¦94251 the¦94256 Levites¦94256 and¦94257 the¦94257 foreigners¦94257 who¦94258 live among you.
\p
\v 12 \add ≈Every¦94267 third¦94272 year¦94271,\add* take a tenth of¦94267 your¦94270 harvest¦94270 to the Levites¦94276, the foreigners¦94277, and the orphans¦94278 and¦94279 widows¦94279 \add ≈in¦94271 your town\add* for them to eat¦94280 and¦94282 be¦94282 content.\x + \xo 26:12: \xt Deu 14:28-29.\x*
